### api/common — the behaviour that belongs to no single module
#
# The error filter, the validation pipe, and the bootstrap-level concerns:
# the version prefix, the not-found fallback, and the body limit. Each of
# these has shipped broken at least once, and each failure was invisible
# from inside the module that appeared to work.

@baseUrl = {{$dotenv baseUrl}}

###
# @name notFound
# 404 {"code":"NOT_FOUND","message":"route not found"} -- JSON, not Express's
# HTML error page. An API that answers HTML on an unmatched path breaks every
# client that parses the body before checking the status.
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/no-such-route

###
# @name notFoundOutsidePrefix
# 404, same JSON body. The fallback covers the whole app, not just /v1.
GET {{baseUrl}}/no-such-route

###
# @name wrongMethod
# 404. /v1/auth/login exists for POST only.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/login

###
# @name missingPrefix
# 404 -- and this is the request that matters.
#
# An earlier catch-all route silently disabled the global prefix, so the API
# answered on *both* /auth/login and /v1/auth/login. Checking only that the
# prefixed path works would not have caught it; this one has to fail.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{$dotenv email}}",
  "password": "{{$dotenv password}}"
}

###
# @name malformedJson
# 400 {"code":"BAD_REQUEST"}. The parser's own error is translated, so a
# syntax error does not surface a stack trace or the parser's internals.
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ this is not json

###
# @name wrongContentType
# 400 VALIDATION_FAILED. Nothing parsed the body, so the schema sees undefined
# and says so, rather than the request reaching the handler with no input.
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: text/plain

email=someone@example.com

###
# @name emptyBody
# 400 naming both missing fields, so a client learns everything wrong with the
# request in one round trip instead of one field at a time.
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### Body limit (API_BODY_LIMIT, 64kb by default)
#
# 413 {"code":"PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE"}, and no rate-limit attempt is recorded:
# the body is refused before the handler runs, so an oversized request cannot
# burn somebody else's login budget. There is no way to write a 200 KB body
# inline that is worth reading, so this one stays a shell command:
#
#   python3 -c "import json,sys; sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({'email':'a@b.co','password':'x'*200000}))" > /tmp/big.json
#   curl -s -w '%{http_code}\n' -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/v1/auth/login \
#     -H 'content-type: application/json' --data-binary @/tmp/big.json
#
# The parser runs before routing, so every route answers the same 413 for an
# oversized JSON body -- the registration routes, and GET /healthz or
# POST /v1/auth/logout too -- before the session guard runs.

###
# @name malformedPathId
# 400 {"code":"BAD_REQUEST"}. Every {id} is a UUID and ParseUUIDPipe refuses
# anything else before the handler, on every operation that takes one (#72,
# defect 4). Needs a session: auth.http's login first.
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/services/not-a-uuid

### Rate limiting (AUTH_MAX_PER_IP, AUTH_MAX_FAILURES_PER_EMAIL)
#
# Both limits are counted, so they need repetition rather than a single
# request. Sending auth.http's loginWrongPassword five times locks the account
# (429 on the sixth, even with the correct password); twenty requests from one
# address exhaust the per-address budget whatever account they name.
#
# The property worth checking is that the limit holds when the requests arrive
# together, which no interactive client can produce:
#
#   for i in $(seq 1 30); do
#     curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/v1/auth/login \
#       -H 'content-type: application/json' \
#       -d "{\"email\":\"conc$i@example.com\",\"password\":\"parallel attempt\"}" &
#   done | sort | uniq -c; wait
#
# Reset the counters between runs with:
#   docker compose exec -T postgres psql -U probeboard -d probeboard -c 'truncate auth_attempts'
